# 🐍 Day 100 — Python Magic Methods / Special Methods

## 📌 What are Magic Methods?

Magic methods (also called **special methods**) are predefined methods in Python that start and end with double underscores (`__`). They allow us to define how our custom objects behave with built-in Python operations.

---

## 🔹 Object Representation

| Magic Method   | One-Line Explanation                                                                                      |
| -------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `__str__()`    | Defines the **user-friendly string representation** of an object, usually used by `print()`.              |
| `__repr__()`   | Defines the **developer-oriented representation** of an object, mainly used for debugging and inspection. |
| `__format__()` | Defines how an object behaves when used with **`format()` or formatted strings** such as `f"{obj}"`.      |

---

## 🔹 Comparison

| Magic Method | One-Line Explanation                                          |
| ------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `__eq__()`   | Defines how two objects are compared using **`==`**.          |
| `__ne__()`   | Defines how two objects are compared using **`!=`**.          |
| `__lt__()`   | Defines comparison using **`<` (less than)**.                 |
| `__le__()`   | Defines comparison using **`<=` (less than or equal to)**.    |
| `__gt__()`   | Defines comparison using **`>` (greater than)**.              |
| `__ge__()`   | Defines comparison using **`>=` (greater than or equal to)**. |

---

## 🧠 Quick Revision

```text
__str__()    → print-friendly representation
__repr__()   → developer/debug representation
__format__() → formatted representation

__eq__() → ==
__ne__() → !=
__lt__() → <
__le__() → <=
__gt__() → >
__ge__() → >=
```
